   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.2. Increased capacity of member States to ensure safe and healthy working conditions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Government adopted comprehensive strategy addressing COVID-19. The strategy includes:
(1) Policies related to the pandemic (Ministry of Manpower Decrees on COVID-19 and Business Continuity Planning), 
(2) High level commitments (several circular letters of the Minister of Manpower on workers&#xBF;f protection, work injury benefit, occupational diseases and the roles of labour inspection during the pandemic), 
(3) Development of two guidelines related to COVID-19 prevention in workplace, i.e. on COVID-19 Prevention and Control at the Workplace and the guidelines for the labour inspection during the COVID-19
(4) Capacity building for labour inspectors and awareness raising/promotion activities (COVID-19 Response Award) Since the start of COVID-19 pandemic, ILO supported the labour inspectors and social partners to implement the health protocol. Key contributions are: 
(1) With support of UNAIDS, the ILO reviewed, provided inputs, and facilitated discussions between government, social partners and Indonesian Medical Association for Occupational Health in developing the guidelines on COVID-19 Prevention and Control at the Workplace. It includes national legal frameworks on COVID-19 prevention and control, steps on business sustainability plan, COVID-19 prevention and control programme at workplace with focuses on preventive, curative and rehabilitative approaches as well as monitoring and evaluation.
(2) With support of Government of Germany, the ILO has been working on a step-by-step OSH action plan for factories to inform and protect workers, by using the latest national guidance as well as ILO and WHO advice, by providing PPE to the factories, and by encouraging the factories to ensure the workers received COVID-19 vaccination. As a result, around 197 factories have instituted temperature screening and social distancing, new handwashing regimes, and distributed masks to workers and necessary health protocol. The ILO facilitated discussion in developing the Labour Inspection Guidelines during the pandemic and supported in distributing the guidelines to all labour inspectors in Indonesia. In addition, the ILO shared good practices from other countries and provided support in the launch of the guidelines for the labour inspection during the COVID-19. 
(3) With support of Government of Japan, the ILO provided technical advice and support in conducting risk assessment and developing response plan during the pandemic.</narrative>
   </description>
