   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.3. Increased institutional capacity of labour administrations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The labour inspectorates of national at the ministry level and three provinces adopted and began implementing strategic compliance plans for improving working conditions in the palm oil sector (South Sumatra, North Sumatra and West Kalimantan).  Sectoral worker and employer organizations contributed to the development of these plans during the planning workshop, sharing their insights with labour inspectors on the industry&#xBF;fs compliance priorities and challenges. In addition, Indonesian Sustainable Palm Oil (ISPO) secretariat including ministry of agriculture representatives collaborated in the planning, helping the inspectorate collaborate with other relevant actors in the sector who monitor working conditions, among others. Subsequent to the adoption of these plans, amid the pandemic and difficult circumstances due to enforced restriction movement by the government of Indonesia, labour inspectors in these provinces conducted a total of 131 inspections visits in fulfilment of the enforcement interventions in the strategic plans. Furthermore, the Directorate General of Labour Inspection and OSH of Ministry of Manpower developed a field guide for conducting labour inspection visits in palm oil plantations and mills sector. The three provincial pilot inspectorates proposed this tool under the strategic plan and then piloted it during inspection visits in the sector. The guide includes sections focusing on priority gender issues such as equal remuneration and particular hazards women workers face in the industry (e.g. pesticide spraying) as well as other decent work deficit such as employment contracts, forced labour, wage, discrimination, and occupational safety and health. It also includes a section to help inspectors address Covid-19 hazards in palm oil operations, thus contributing to the country&#xBF;fs Covid-19 response. With support from the USDRL (US Department of State, Bureau for Democracy, Human Rights and Labour), the ILO conducted a strategic compliance workshop in March 2020 for the three pilot provinces above, which led to the adoption of the strategic plans. The ILO also assisted in the drafting of the palm oil field guide for inspectors and convened multiple inspector consultations to adapt the guide to relevant national legislation and inspection protocols in Indonesia. Inspectors also used the ILO&#xBF;fs online Strategic Compliance Portal to monitor and track results of the plans.</narrative>
   </description>
